## Task
@@README_GOAL@@ AM-Bench environment `@@ENV_ID@@` (the fully actuated hexarotor with the 4-joint arm, AM-Bench's PID
controller), its seeded reset with seed @@SEED@@. The deliverable is one replayable trajectory of AM-Bench's absolute
end-effector pose actions at 120 Hz (`/app/output/trajectory.npz`), graded by AM-Bench's own success check within the
task's episode length of @@EPISODE_S@@ s.

## Difficulty
@@DIFFICULTY@@ Privileged mode gives the full simulator state, unlimited resets and self-replay; the tooling offers only the
environment's own action, its state and its cameras.

## Reference solution
`solution/oracle_actions.npy` (run by `solution/oracle.py` through the agent's own tooling): AM-Bench's scripted expert
for this task, run on this instance (it reads the simulator's state in closed loop) and recorded, followed by 2 s of
holding its last command (a replay may succeed a step later than the live run; rows after success never run). The image
has no expert (`images/ambench/Dockerfile` removes them), and the agent's network reaches only the
model APIs and the package registries.

## Verification
`tests/grade.py` in a separate sandbox: two replays, each in a fresh process, from the verifier's own copy of the instance
(`tests/instance.json`; the rebuilt start is checked against its hash); pass iff AM-Bench's success check holds in both
and both end in the same state (`deterministic`); the replays run without the onboard cameras (camera rendering makes
GPU PhysX timing-dependent). `/logs/verifier/replay.mp4` (both onboard cameras) is filmed by a third process after the
verdict, an illustration only. Oracle:
success at control step @@N_ORACLE@@ (@@ORACLE_SIM_S@@ s simulated) in both fresh-process replays, which end in the same
state (hash `@@ORACLE_SHA@@`). `nop`: no trajectory, reward 0.

